WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:04.620
There are certain things in&nbsp;
life that are universally known.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:10.680
There are 8 planets in the solar system, the&nbsp;
blue whale is the biggest animal that ever lived,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:14.340
diamond is the hardest naturally&nbsp;
occurring substance on earth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:22.980
and Mount Everest is the tallest mountain. But&nbsp;
my dear viewer, that last fact is a total lie.

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:27.120
And if you think this video is just going&nbsp;
to be about a certain Hawaiian volcano,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:33.960
you’re only half right. What if I was to tell&nbsp;
you there are in fact two mountains other than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:39.060
Everest that can quite reasonably&nbsp;
claim to be the tallest on earth.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:44.040
You see, despite the fact that every&nbsp;
textbook, website, encyclopedia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:50.280
and natural history documentary you’ve ever seen&nbsp;
has confidently told you that Mount Everest is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:55.455
the tallest peak on the planet… I‘ve got&nbsp;
some surprising news for you: it isn‘t.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:22.500
In 1802, the East India Company launched&nbsp;
one of the biggest cartographical projects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:27.000
in history. Known as the Great&nbsp;
Trigonometrical Survey, it aimed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:33.060
to precisely map all four and a half million&nbsp;
square kilometers of the Indian Subcontinent.

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:38.040
Over the course of almost 70 years,&nbsp;
the Great Trigonometrical Survey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:42.540
quite literally put India on the map,&nbsp;
and one of its crowning achievements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:47.100
was to determine the exact heights of&nbsp;
many of the tallest mountains on Earth.

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:51.960
This was only 200 or so years ago - just&nbsp;
a few generations back - but in those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:57.240
days many of these most massive of mountains&nbsp;
were completely unknown to western science.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:02.760
It wasn‘t just that we weren‘t sure how tall&nbsp;
they were - we didn’t even know they existed.

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:10.020
That made the Great Trigonometrical Survey a true&nbsp;
journey of discovery, and as its cartographers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:16.440
explorers, and surveyors penetrated ever deeper&nbsp;
into the remotest reaches of the Himalayas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:22.560
they met ever more monstrous mountains.&nbsp;
As a result, the mountain considered to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:27.480
the tallest on earth changed on a fairly&nbsp;
regular basis throughout the nineteenth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:32.520
century as new peaks were discovered and&nbsp;
accurately measured for the first time.

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:40.080
In the 300 years or so preceding the survey it&nbsp;
had been widely accepted that Mount Chimborazo in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:46.080
Ecuador was the tallest mountain in the world when&nbsp;
measured against sea level. That changed in 1808&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:51.480
when the Survey discovered a Nepalese mountain&nbsp;
called Dhaulagiri which wasn‘t just bigger than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:57.120
Chimborazo, it was *much* bigger - almost 2 whole&nbsp;
kilometres. Dhaulagiri was considered the tallest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:03.300
mountain on earth until 1847, when Kangchenjunga&nbsp;
was found to be a couple of hundred metres taller,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:11.820
and Kangchenjunga passed the baton on to K2 when&nbsp;
*it* was discovered in 1856. K2 only held the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:17.280
record for a matter of months, because later that&nbsp;
year Mount Everest - named after George Everest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:22.920
one-time head of the Great Trigonometrical&nbsp;
Survey and Surveyor General of India - was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:29.760
officially declared the tallest mountain in&nbsp;
the world. It's held that title ever since.

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:35.220
But despite more than 160 years&nbsp;
as the world's premier peak,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:39.960
Mount Everest‘s hold on that 'lofty'&nbsp;
record is surprisingly shaky.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:45.540
The question is, if Everest isn‘t the&nbsp;
true king of the mountains… what is?

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:48.660
Confusingly, there isn’t actually a simple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:52.500
answer to that question - it all&nbsp;
comes down to how you measure it.

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:57.300
And sure enough, if you were to hold up&nbsp;
the world's longest tape measure against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:02.940
every mountain on Earth you would indeed&nbsp;
find that Mount Everest is the tallest.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:10.020
But you just made the classic mountain-measuring&nbsp;
mistake of standing on dry land.

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:16.020
Because the honour of highest hill&nbsp;
actually belongs to Mauna Kea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:21.960
a dormant volcano that towers over Hawai'i,&nbsp;
the largest of the Hawaiian islands. And Mauna&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:27.780
Kea doesn't pip Everest by a hair either-&nbsp;
standing at a staggering 10,210 metres,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:33.840
it's well over a kilometre taller. If you&nbsp;
were to drop Britain‘s tallest mountain - Ben&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:40.080
Nevis - directly on top of Everest‘s snowy&nbsp;
peak, Mauna Kea would *still* be bigger.

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:44.700
There is, however, a bit of a catch -&nbsp;
more than half of the mighty mountain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:50.700
Mauna Kea is submerged beneath the Pacific&nbsp;
Ocean, leaving a little over 4,200 metres&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:55.860
above sea level - that's why Everest is&nbsp;
officially considered the taller mountain.

00:05:55.860 --> 00:06:00.420
And you may think that sounds perfectly&nbsp;
reasonable... but is it *really.*

00:06:00.420 --> 00:06:06.360
Standing at 8 feet 3 inches, Turkish&nbsp;
farmer Sultan Kösen is the tallest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:11.400
man alive today. If he was to, say,&nbsp;
stand in a swimming pool for a bit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:16.440
would that change his height? Would the&nbsp;
good people at Guinness give his record&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:23.400
to someone else? Obviously not. So why&nbsp;
doesn't the same logic apply to mountains?

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:28.920
You could argue that a Turkish farmer going&nbsp;
for a dip isn't quite the same as Mauna Kea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:33.660
standing submerged in the Pacific Ocean ever&nbsp;
since it formed about a million years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:37.980
and that's a fair point. But&nbsp;
not even oceans are permanent.

00:06:37.980 --> 00:06:43.080
Admittedly, climbing Mauna Kea is going to be a&nbsp;
very different experience to scaling Mount Everest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:48.300
(you're going to need access to a submarine&nbsp;
for one thing). And in contrast to the rarified&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:53.700
air of Everest's upper slopes, the peak of&nbsp;
Mauna Kea isn't even always covered in snow.

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:59.220
Perhaps that's the real reason Mount Everest&nbsp;
is so widely accepted as the big daddy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:03.780
Mauna is clearly taller, but Everest&nbsp;
pierces higher into the atmosphere.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:09.180
When you reach its peak you are quite&nbsp;
literally standing on top of the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:13.620
the closest you can get to the stars&nbsp;
with your feet on solid ground...

00:07:13.620 --> 00:07:17.700
... except, that isn't actually true either.

00:07:17.700 --> 00:07:21.300
We’ve already established that Everest&nbsp;
isn‘t the tallest mountain on earth if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:24.420
you measure from base to tip (and&nbsp;
you should always measure base&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.420 --> 00:07:29.580
to tip). But what if I was to tell&nbsp;
you that you can climb even higher?

00:07:29.580 --> 00:07:35.160
Unless you're Kyrie Irving, you probably&nbsp;
picture Planet Earth as a giant sphere.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:39.780
That's how it's always depicted, and that's&nbsp;
how it appears when we see images of it from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:46.200
space. But that isn't entirely accurate: in&nbsp;
reality, our homeworld is an oblate spheroid.

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:49.920
To put that into normal person&nbsp;
English, earth isn't spherical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:56.640
nor is it flat - it's *fat:* thanks to the&nbsp;
combined effects of gravity and earth's spin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:01.620
like your waistline after Christmas dinner,&nbsp;
our planet bulges around the middle.

00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:07.440
On a planetary scale that bulge is absolutely&nbsp;
tiny - if you were to shrink earth down to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:11.460
the size of a beach ball, it would be&nbsp;
about a millimetre wider when measured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:15.600
around the equator rather than through&nbsp;
the poles (that‘s why you can‘t detect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:21.060
the bulge when you see images of earth from&nbsp;
space). But on something the size of a planet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:27.240
even a minuscule deformation has a&nbsp;
significant impact on a human scale.

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:32.280
Someone standing at the North Pole is about&nbsp;
20 kilometres closer to earth’s core than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:36.480
someone standing on the equator. And the&nbsp;
same, of course, is true for mountains.

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:39.720
Thanks to a boost from earth’s unsightly paunch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:45.360
the highest point on earth when measured from&nbsp;
the centre of the planet isn't on top of Mount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:51.840
Everest. It isn't even in the Himalayas&nbsp;
- it's 10,000 miles away in Ecuador.

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:55.920
I mentioned at the start of this&nbsp;
video that, for several hundred years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.920 --> 00:09:00.600
Mount Chimborazo in Ecuador was thought to be&nbsp;
the tallest mountain in the world when measured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:06.180
against sea level. Mountaineers today flock&nbsp;
to Mount Everest, but in the 17th and 18th&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:12.420
centuries it was Chimborazo that brought in crowds&nbsp;
of climbers hoping to stand on top of the world.

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:17.940
In reality, western science had gotten&nbsp;
this one quite spectacularly wrong - with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:23.040
an elevation of just 6,263 metres,&nbsp;
Chimborazo doesn’t even make the top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:28.500
200 tallest mountains on earth - it’s&nbsp;
only the 39th tallest in the Andes.

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:34.680
But in a genuinely incredible coincidence,&nbsp;
thanks to its location just one degree south&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.680 --> 00:09:42.540
of the equator, Chimborazo *is* the *highest*&nbsp;
point on the planet. Its peak is a healthy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.540 --> 00:09:49.860
3,967 miles from earth‘s centre, a full 1.3&nbsp;
miles higher than the peak of Mount Everest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:56.880
despite being more than 2.5 kilometres shorter&nbsp;
when measured against sea level. So in the end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.880 --> 00:10:02.640
all those 17th and 18th century mountaineers&nbsp;
who made it to the peak of Chimborazo really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:07.260
*were* standing on top of the world&nbsp;
- just not in the way they thought.

00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:12.000
So, Everest, Mauna Kea, or Chimborazo?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.540 --> 00:10:19.380
Ultimately it's up to you. But there's a very real&nbsp;
argument that of these three mountains, Everest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.380 --> 00:10:26.400
actually has the *least* claim to the title of&nbsp;
the tallest on earth. Mauna Kea is objectively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:33.120
the 'biggest,‘ and Chimborazo is indisputably&nbsp;
the 'highest‘. By comparison, Everest's record&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:40.380
as the highest peak above sea level starts to&nbsp;
sound kind of niche, even a little arbitrary.

00:10:40.380 --> 00:10:43.680
Don't get me wrong, Everest remains a majestic and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.680 --> 00:10:48.300
inspiring place. It pierces further into&nbsp;
the atmosphere than any other mountain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.300 --> 00:10:54.660
making its peak one of the most extreme places&nbsp;
ever visited by man. It has an incredible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.660 --> 00:11:00.360
history and its status as a mountain of&nbsp;
legendary proportions is well deserved.

00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:02.940
But that doesn‘t change the fact that&nbsp;
there are several perfectly reasonable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.940 --> 00:11:09.360
ways of measuring the tallest mountain on&nbsp;
earth, and Everest only wins one of them.

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:11.340
Thanks for watching.

